{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\n  \"records\": [\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S191-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"581bc27e51743920d701b2d42cc9fe98915ba0e330bb48f00782b39aa5a6bdb8\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 identifies one misplaced dot; A3 makes the marriage's standing depend on typography.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2's twenty years with 'Socially'; A5 extends B4's children into inheritance.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 defend their shared marriage through duration and children.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B4 prompts A5 to extend the supposed defect into the next generation.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1's marriage certificate and B2's twenty years immediately establish spouses and stakes.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'Socially, certainly' efficiently preserves sincerity; A5's inheritance follows B4.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 88\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"This marriage certificate has the dot above the wrong letter.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"We've been married twenty years.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Twenty years together. But until that dot moves, this doesn't certify a marriage.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Then move it. Our anniversary party starts at seven.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Certainly. But we'll need a wedding cake. This dot makes tonight our first day.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A treats one misplaced mark as the sole source of a marriage's official legitimacy despite twenty years together.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Ordinary proofreading would correct the certificate without changing when A believes the marriage begins.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 withholds certification until the dot moves; A5 treats its correction as converting tonight's anniversary into the first wedding day.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This alternative lets a spouse's practical correction change a shared celebration; alternative b lets a departing member's correction expose retrospective club liability.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised the rejected candidate within the marriage setting, adding an imminent anniversary party. Internal reread under coach-1.0: fidelity 5, because A3 and A5 make the dot determine certification and commencement; logic 5, because A3 separates B2's duration from validity and A5 converts B4's anniversary after the requested correction; relationship 4, because B4 wants the paperwork fixed before their party; heightening 4, because A5 changes the celebration's purpose and cake; specificity 5, because A1's certificate and B4's seven-o'clock party give playable circumstances; brevity 4, because A5's wedding cake follows the newly valid marriage. Total 92. Five spoken ABABA turns, under 75 words; the celebration remains open to negotiation. Original firstPass preserved.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A's belief that correcting the mark starts the marriage is deliberately extreme fictional logic; the new cake makes that belief concrete without establishing actual legal procedure.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S191-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"a16fbf028c86156fca660011290cf55c956c5819b31ec718b6e0fa79e6cbf1c9\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 and A3 hinge on seal orientation, but A5 substitutes an international-form requirement.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"B4 proposes turning the page; A5 gives no coherent reason this makes the submission international.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 wants departure acknowledged; B4 offers a practical fix to A's obstruction.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 adds another obstacle, but abandons the particular defect established in A1.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes a club resignation and upside-down seal; B2 establishes distance.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'Travel doesn't rotate a seal' is earned; A5's international classification is arbitrary.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 61\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Your club resignation has the seal upside down. You're still our secretary.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I've moved abroad. Rotate the page.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"There. Now your resignation is valid.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Good. You can stop sending me the minutes.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I'll rotate the previous eleven too. We may owe you a year's subscriptions.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A lets the orientation of one seal determine whether a resignation has official force.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"General bureaucratic obstruction can invent successive requirements instead of remaining committed to one validating detail.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A5 changes requirements; revised A3 accepts the rotated seal and A5 applies that same criterion retrospectively.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a fears an inherited family defect; this version lets a departing member's helpful fix expose the club's financial liability.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised within the club setting. Internal second pass: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 4, brevity 4 = 90. B4's request to stop the minutes makes A5 examine previous resignations and extend the correction to subscriptions. Five spoken turns, under 75 words.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 introduces eleven previous submissions; that is playable escalation but asks actors to absorb an unseen correspondence history.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S192-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"058d1ebedf34a070871d50a821d0c6ff89000a0bc22e485e5b1a6b01754ec3b6\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 converts an empty-chair coincidence into allocation doctrine; A5 converts kindness into entitlement.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"B2 supplies availability for A3; B4 supplies reservation for A5's revised policy.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 corrects A's assumption, then B4 voluntarily offers future companionship.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 upgrades accidental proximity into an ongoing reserved entitlement because of B4.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes lunch seating; B2 makes the initial encounter intelligible.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'Useful clarification' and A5's entitlement efficiently turn warmth into doctrine.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 90\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You sat beside me at lunch. Shall I record that as our friendship policy?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"It was the only empty chair.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"So friendship is allocated by availability. Useful clarification.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Tomorrow I'll save you a seat.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Excellent. We've moved from allocation to a reserved entitlement.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A interprets accidental seating and a personal offer as successive versions of an institutional friendship policy.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"A simple appetite for rules would prescribe behavior without deriving doctrine from B's actual choices.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 derives allocation from B2's empty chair; A5 updates the doctrine when B4 offers a reserved seat.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This alternative institutionalizes a growing friendship through helpful offers; alternative b institutionalizes a personal act of repair.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained after rereading because B's kindness changes the policy and advances the relationship without requiring an argument.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A1 announces the policy vocabulary immediately, leaving little gradual discovery of A's interpretive habit.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S192-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"464fb6b1bc6225c835025944f6910ac819ffa7899542c63e6dbc08db9a8ad3c1\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 makes apology and tea a procedure; A3 derives a damage rule from B2.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 uses B2's broken cup; A5 incorporates B4's remorse as documentation.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 explains the accident and B4 asserts feeling, but neither strongly changes available choices.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 adds a document metaphor without showing a larger consequence of A3's doctrine.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1's tea and B2's knocked-over cup establish a domestic repair.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 compresses the mistaken causal rule; A5's optional remorse follows B4's feeling.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 80\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You apologised before making tea. Is that our conflict-resolution procedure now?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I knocked over your cup.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"So material damage triggers refreshments.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Yesterday I made tea because you looked sad. Nothing broke.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then sadness qualifies as damage. I'll amend the procedure before either of us has to smash anything.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A connects personal acts of care into a general institutional rule and incorporates exceptions by amending its categories.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Bureaucratic vocabulary alone decorates a conversation without making personal evidence generate a larger system.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised B4 supplies a second tea-making occasion; A5 expands the damage category to reconcile both occasions.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a turns a new friendship into an entitlement; this version makes a household care policy broad enough to cover emotional distress.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised in the existing tea setting. Internal second pass: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 4, brevity 4 = 90. B4's counterexample causes A5's wider doctrine and practical commitment. Five spoken turns, under 75 words.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5's concern about smashing things is an exaggerated implication, though it follows the newly established damage rule.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S193-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"ec5554903251639c749aa708da47c26899401b0b4e1a2e02a21cc43c80e27cba\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 claims victory authorizes a favorable theme; A3 claims authority over fairness.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2's fairness objection, but A5 merely repeats B4's half-point as a committee name.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 objects generally; B4 supplies a margin but no concrete alternative or consequence.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 claims another chairmanship without showing what the half-point committee controls.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1's bake-off and familiar cakes are playable; A5's committee remit is unclear.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3's leadership slogan is concise, but A5's committee feels assembled from B4's noun.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 68\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I won the bake-off, so next year's theme is cakes I already make.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"You beat my lemon cake by half a point.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then lemon cakes clearly get a fair chance under my leadership.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Fine. I'll enter that same cake again.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Excellent. Returning challengers must improve by a full point. The champion sets the standard.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A treats winning as authority to define the next competition and the fairness of rules that protect the winner.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Ordinary competitive cheating seeks an advantage without treating previous success as a mandate to govern.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1 invokes the win as authority, and revised A5 explicitly makes the champion the source of the next standard.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This alternative changes competitive eligibility against a close rival; alternative b extends a family victory into control over its reward.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised within the bake-off setting. Internal second pass: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 4, brevity 4 = 90. B4's decision to reenter the near-winning cake causes A5 to impose a larger improvement requirement. Five spoken turns, under 75 words.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The rule is transparently self-serving; A needs sincere confidence in the champion's authority rather than a conspiratorial delivery.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S193-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"86c9c543785f90713428ef82aa1a29de620af994bdd4b7065e6ce5716304d610\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 converts bowling victory into dinner authority; A5 explicitly claims the right to define the next event.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 reinterprets B2's trophy, and A5 uses B4's exclusion of dinner as an opening for new terms.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 both repeat the original agreement rather than adding a materially different pressure.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 restates winner authority without naming a new event or increasing the practical demand.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes family bowling and dinner; B2 specifies the agreed trophy.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'judgement under pressure' is apt, but A5 ends in an abstract statement of the card.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 78\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"As winner of family bowling, I choose where we eat.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"We only agreed you'd get a trophy.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"A trophy recognises judgement under pressure. Mine says pizza.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Your brother can't eat cheese.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then the next event is finding pizza he can eat. I'll award myself the biggest slice for organising it.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A uses a prior success as a license to expand the reward and set the next family challenge.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Generic bossiness demands dinner control without deriving that authority from a particular win.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1 anchors authority in bowling; A3 interprets the trophy as judgment credentials; A5 adds a winner-designed event and reward.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a protects a champion against a close rival; this version accommodates a family constraint while awarding A more authority and pizza.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised within family bowling and dinner. Internal second pass: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 4, brevity 4 = 90. B4's dietary constraint causes A5's new search event and self-awarded reward. Five spoken turns, under 75 words.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The cheese constraint is deliberately brief; the scene leaves the actual meal and brother's response open.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S194-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"7ace755fc6736743fa5062db6c4d5c5d919006bf832847bdd1bacf832c11343d\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 dismisses defeat and stakes reputation on a rematch; A5's tea contest loses that explicit standing anxiety.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 uses B2's thirty years; A5 turns B4's wish for tea into a replacement contest.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 offers long acquaintance as reassurance; B4 wants ordinary companionship over tea.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 changes the competition to tea but does not raise what its result means for A's standing.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes a lost chess game; B2 and B4 establish a longstanding social ritual.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5's 'Best tea wins' follows B4 cleanly, although it primarily demonstrates competitiveness.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 77\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"That lost chess game was practice. This rematch will establish my reputation.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"We've played here for thirty years.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Exactly. I won't have thirty years remembered for one dropped rook.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I just came for tea.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then I'll make it. One excellent pot and you'll remember why you keep coming back.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A projects control while treating a loss as a threat to longstanding importance and making the next result responsible for restoring it.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Simple competitiveness would turn tea into another contest without fearing the loss of B's regard.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A3 fears thirty years being reduced to a dropped rook; A5 makes tea prove why B should keep visiting.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This alternative shifts threatened standing into a friendship ritual; alternative b removes karaoke equipment and raises the exposure of A's performance.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised within the chess-and-tea setting. Internal second pass: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 4, brevity 4 = 90. B4's wish for tea makes the next pot responsible for validating the whole friendship. Five spoken turns, under 75 words.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A3 exposes anxiety more directly than the original; confident delivery must preserve the instruction's outward assurance.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S194-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"e1c43a7ede3169e19c33783ab96e8dd6287564adf40b63e5eb0e84661a7f4f83\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 stakes continued ability on the next song after a bad score; A3 insists on flawless proof despite reassurance.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 uses B2's absence of doubt as something to preserve; A5 turns B4's unplugging into an unmediated test.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 reassures A, while B4 removes the expected scoring apparatus and performance route.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 makes the exposed voice itself the test once B4 removes the machine.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1's karaoke score and B4's unplugged machine provide immediate playable circumstances.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'keep it that way' turns kindness into pressure; A5 makes the obstacle an opportunity.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 90\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Ignore my terrible karaoke score. The next song determines whether I still have it.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Nobody doubted you. It's just closing time.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Excellent. One flawless song will keep it that way.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I unplugged it. I'm closing; your friends have gone.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Perfect. One unaccompanied chorus will decide whether you book me for next Friday or forget my name.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A confidently turns each setback into a more consequential test of whether A's standing will survive.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Optimistic persistence seeks another performance without making that performance decide continued recognition and future inclusion.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 makes flawlessness necessary despite B2's reassurance; A5 treats B4's closing and departed audience as a decisive audition against being forgotten.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a makes excellent tea justify a friend's continued visits; this version turns a closing host into the sole judge of A's future booking and recognition.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised the rejected karaoke candidate by making B the closing host and giving the final performance explicit future stakes. Internal reread under coach-1.0: fidelity 5, because A1 dismisses failure and A5 confidently risks both next Friday and recognition; logic 5, because A3 preserves B2's absence of doubt and A5 substitutes an unaccompanied audition after B4 disconnects the machine and removes the audience; relationship 4, because B2 and B4 want to close; heightening 4, because A5 makes the remaining chorus determine future access; specificity 4, because A1 and B4 establish karaoke at closing; brevity 4, because A5's confident 'Perfect' directly reinterprets B4's setback. Total 90. Five spoken ABABA turns, under 75 words; B has not agreed to listen or book A. Original firstPass preserved.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A invents the audition's stakes without B endorsing them. That supports the self-imposed pressure, although the possibility of a Friday booking is introduced only in A5.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S195-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"c1c32619d0984537b76388c0578d5119ae7eddb4cc2041688bc4652dd3cd998d\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 demands unstretched measurement and A3 rejects visual judgment; A5 asserts expertise without correcting the method.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B4 identifies a knitted table cover, but A5 leaves unclear how knowing its stretch validates a calibrated table.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 is a rival claimant, and B4 challenges A's measuring apparatus through A's own handiwork.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 defends A's knowledge but adds no precision or consequence to the measurement.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes scarf length clearly; A3's calibrated table and B4's cover make the apparatus hard to picture.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 uses B4's knitting fact, but the stretch explanation needs an unstated measurement procedure.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 69\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You can't claim longest scarf until we've measured without stretching it.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"It's obviously longer than yours.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then you won't mind laying both flat against this tape.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Yours only reaches because of the tassels.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Quite right. Separate fringe measurements. I may have to surrender overall length and retain decorative reach.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A makes precise measurement and category distinctions a prerequisite for recognizing either competitor's success.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Sore-loser obstruction disputes results to preserve victory without showing an actual commitment to measurement craft.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A5 accepts B4's tassel objection and allows a loss in overall length while distinguishing decorative reach.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This alternative tests the scorer's own entry against a rival; alternative b adapts certification when a cake's evidence has vanished.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised within the scarf contest, replacing the unclear table with a tape. Internal second pass: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 5, brevity 4 = 92. B4's fringe objection causes A5's separate measurements and possible concession. Five spoken turns, under 75 words.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"Decorative reach is A's proposed category, so performance should emphasize exact classification rather than a transparent consolation prize.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S195-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"121ca912d1122b130a45b38b8556eaedf8841503b7d46c4dffb57cc1da601697\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 asks for a scoring scale, A3 rejects inconsistent judging, and A5 restricts what can be certified.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 challenges B2's love as a measure; A5 replaces presentation after B4 says the cake was eaten.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 defends the cake through approval; B4 makes A's requested visual assessment unavailable.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 narrows the entire certifiable result to consumption because B4 removes the evidence.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes a scored cake; B4 supplies the concrete complication of its disappearance.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's consistency objection is concise; A5's consumption category grows directly from B4.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 90\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Before I award your cake best presentation, I need all three judges' scores.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"They all said ten. I've already framed 'perfect cake'.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Ten out of what? I need matching scales before I average them.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Two meant ten out of ten. The third meant ten out of twenty.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then eight and a third out of ten. Your 'perfect cake' certificate needs a fraction.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A corrects the scoring scale and aggregate before permitting B to present a cake's success as officially perfect.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Generic pedantry corrects a number without making the correction govern what achievement someone may claim.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 requires comparable denominators before averaging; A5 converts B4's mixed scales into a precise result that requires changing B2's framed claim.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a makes A accept a rival's correction to A's own scarf; this version makes a cake entrant's premature celebration answer to a scorer's calculation.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised the rejected candidate within cake judging, replacing vanished evidence with incompatible scoring scales and a premature certificate. Internal reread under coach-1.0: fidelity 5, because A3 normalizes standards before A5 permits a quantified claim; logic 5, because B2's tens cause A3's denominator question and B4's scales yield A5's eight-and-a-third average; relationship 4, because B2 has already framed the desired result; heightening 4, because A5 requires that visible celebration to change; specificity 4, because A1 identifies an award and three judges; brevity 4, because A5's fraction answers both B4's figures and B2's perfection claim. Total 90. Five spoken ABABA turns, under 75 words. Original firstPass preserved.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The calculation assumes equally weighted judges, as A3's proposed average implies. The dialogue corrects the perfection claim but leaves whether the cake wins best presentation unresolved.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S196-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"780cab4481a7b98c1caa5718a6e1ccd3d51aec41c9825c6916cfba29a82ed07d\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 offers balanced accounts before an invitation challenge; A5 offers another competence display after rejection.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3 says votes have already been counted after B2 says a vote is needed, without establishing that one occurred.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 defend the joining procedure, but B4 supplies no new practical obstacle.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 expands into process design, though the claim that joining loses money is unsupported.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1's committee accounts and B2's membership vote establish a clear participation dispute.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 efficiently exposes the need to earn admission; A5's money claim feels attached for the closing line.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 74\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Before you ask who invited me to the committee, I've balanced its accounts.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"You still need a membership vote.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Could you circulate the accounts with my application? Especially the savings.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"We can't vote tonight. Nobody's taken the minutes.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I have. Alphabetised, with action points. Shall I read the section where you need a treasurer?\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A responds to uncertain membership by making completed, useful work increasingly visible as evidence of deserving a place.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"A power grab seeks committee control; this behavior uses competence to establish a right to participate.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A3 attaches work to a membership application, and A5 answers a procedural delay with another completed service.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This alternative meets a formal admission barrier with documentary competence; alternative b turns a welcoming invitation into self-imposed performance duties.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised within the committee setting. Internal second pass: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 4, brevity 4 = 90. B4's missing minutes cause A5 to reveal a second completed task and press the treasurer need. Five spoken turns, under 75 words.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The minutes requirement is this fictional committee's practice; A5's treasurer opening remains opportunistic rather than proof of admission.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S196-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"0bfec3bf1d3d6967d91864de988df57ebaf9891194e5b681aac0ea051dfcb36b\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 offers repaired lights for inclusion; A3 turns an open rehearsal invitation into learning everyone's lines.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 uses B2's rehearsal offer; A5 takes B4's audience role as the next thing to excel at.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 welcomes A without a test, and B4 explicitly removes the perceived audition requirement.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 promises to be the best audience but provides no visible act comparable to A3's learning all lines.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1's stage lights and B2's rehearsal establish a theatre group and an aspiring participant.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B4's audience correction neatly prompts A5, though 'best audience' remains a general label.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 83\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I've repaired the stage lights. Is there room for me in the theatre group now?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"You can just come to a rehearsal.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I'll learn everyone's lines first.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"You don't have to audition for the audience.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Of course. I'll time my laughs so nobody loses a line. You'll want me in the front row.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A converts unconditional welcome into another opportunity to prove that belonging has been earned through exceptional performance.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Ordinary helpful enthusiasm offers assistance without treating it as the justification for being allowed to stay.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1 explicitly trades repairs for room in the group; revised A5 makes expert audience behavior a reason to want A seated prominently.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a faces an actual vote requirement; here B repeatedly welcomes A and A supplies the admission tests.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised in the theatre setting. Internal second pass: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 4, brevity 4 = 90. B4 removes auditioning, so A5 makes audience timing the next demonstrable skill. Five spoken turns, under 75 words.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The new ending is more visibly performative but should retain A's need for acceptance rather than become a boast about comic timing.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S197-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"b41015d45612c9b28f4a2be5ad498cd5853ec9a27a81337058e9f3b3daedb07b\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 praises the serve while claiming drills; A3 and A5 preserve coaching seniority despite contrary facts.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"B2's lack of training becomes observational teaching in A3; B4's actual model becomes A's defeat-as-lesson in A5.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 refuses a false coaching history, and B4 identifies the person whose technique B actually copied.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A5 claims instructional credit for being beaten after B4 makes A's failure the apparent disproof.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes a winning serve; B4 supplies a concrete competitive history.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A5's 'my defeat provided the lesson' resolves B4's exact contradiction without extra explanation.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 95\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Lovely winning serve. My drills finally paid off.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I've never trained with you.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You watched me practise. Observation is powerful teaching.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I copied the person who beat you.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then my defeat provided the lesson. You're welcome.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A praises B's skill while absorbing each correction into a broader claim that A supplied its teaching method.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"General credit stealing claims involvement without maintaining the particular senior role of coach or teacher.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 explicitly calls observation teaching; A5 turns A's defeat into a lesson that explains B's winning serve.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This alternative claims passive demonstration despite no training relationship; alternative b recasts actively wrong advice as sophisticated coaching.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained after rereading: B's increasingly precise corrections require distinct reinterpretations, and the final claim specifically needs the revelation of A's defeat.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The sport is unspecified, and A3 assumes B watched practice; both are small, playable contextual gaps.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S197-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"eea6816e1b288f13be23115c01c16f8cb2993312ffafb26686f78644e9d9e816\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 praises the discount while claiming influence; A3 interprets wrong advice as training independent judgment.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 uses B2's full-price advice; A5 answers B4's interpretation but largely restates A3.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 challenges A with a concrete contradiction; B4 mainly summarizes the resulting claim.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5 labels the same explanation 'Advanced coaching' without a new consequence or stronger claim.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes a negotiated discount and B2 the conflicting advice, though the purchase is unspecified.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's provoked independence is economical; A5's polished maxim repeats its underlying explanation.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 77\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You negotiated a brilliant discount on that sofa. That's my influence.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"You told me to pay full price.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I was provoking independent judgement.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Next time I'll leave you at home.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Excellent. You're ready for unsupervised work. Ring me afterwards for your assessment.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A treats B's successful independence, including rejection of A's help, as progress through A's coaching.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Defensive excuse-making protects A from being wrong without necessarily praising B or claiming ongoing instructional seniority.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1 praises the discount; revised A5 praises exclusion as readiness while reserving the right to assess B.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a claims credit through a witnessed defeat; this version claims that bad advice and future exclusion are planned stages of tuition.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised by specifying a sofa purchase within the existing negotiation situation. Internal second pass: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 4, brevity 4 = 90. B4 withdraws access, causing A5 to invent unsupervised progression and retain assessment authority. Five spoken turns, under 75 words.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The assessment request is an illustrative character demand and leaves B free to reject the claimed coaching relationship.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S198-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"807d4de72f35daf5e6c691e8c79d664c41d6aa8e1156c1dad174a048e7dac027\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 controls a prize box, but A3 and A5 never specify a condition that determines the value of winning.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3 distinguishes ticket and prize after B2; A5 evades B4's question without changing the negotiation.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 asserts ownership and B4 seeks information, but A's unspecified conditions prevent concrete bargaining.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5 repeats A1's withholding without increasing the cost, deadline, or consequence.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes a raffle, but A5 leaves both reward and obligations opaque.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3's different responsibilities is compact but abstract; A5 returns to the same unexplained conditions.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 57\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Congratulations on winning the raffle. The prize is this hamper, once you've returned the basket.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I need the basket to carry everything home.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You can borrow it until six. I'll keep the chocolates as a deposit.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"My bus doesn't get back until seven.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Seven is fine. I'll need the wine as well.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A quietly determines the practical value of a win by controlling collection conditions and the reward held back against them.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Mere secrecy conceals a prize without exercising concrete control over what the winner can receive.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A3 withholds chocolates against basket return; A5 accepts a later deadline while increasing the retained reward.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This alternative bargains over a physical hamper and return time; alternative b controls access to a parking reward through scheduling.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised within the raffle setting, specifying the former mystery prize as a hamper. Internal second pass: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 5, brevity 4 = 92. B4's later bus causes A5 to grant time while withholding wine. Five spoken turns, under 75 words.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The escalating deposit makes A's administration increasingly onerous; the understated delivery should keep it from becoming overt intimidation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S198-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"a91e19db5911a263adc0ce9d863705878a17713e6b166c8198cacc62b912b7cd\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 controls the allocation calendar; A3 makes the requested day premium, limiting the reward.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 addresses B2's tomorrow request; A5 responds to B4's free-space promise through timing.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 seeks actual access and B4 invokes the advertised terms against A's restriction.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5 says timing remains unresolved without imposing or revealing a consequential allocation.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1's office parking prize and B2's tomorrow request give the exchange an immediate practical purpose.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5's 'when freedom applies' is more abstract than the calendar dispute warrants.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 72\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You've won five hours of office parking. I'll issue the permit.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Great. I start at nine tomorrow.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Certainly. One free hour each morning.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"My shift is eight hours. I can't keep moving the car.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I can combine them for tomorrow. You'll only owe me for the last three hours.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A calmly controls how a fixed parking reward is allocated, determining whether winning provides useful access or leads to a bill.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Routine scheduling supplies a requested time without making A's allocation determine the reward's practical cost and coverage.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 spreads the prize across mornings; A5 responds to B4's full shift by spending the whole prize on one day and identifying the remaining charge.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a grants extra time while retaining more hamper contents; this version helpfully consolidates time while exhausting the prize and exposing a payable remainder.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised the rejected parking candidate within the office setting, specifying five prize hours and an eight-hour shift instead of assuming Sunday is unusable. Internal reread under coach-1.0: fidelity 5, because A3 and A5 quietly set allocation and payment terms; logic 5, because A3 accommodates B2's morning start and A5 uses B4's eight hours to combine five free hours with three paid hours; relationship 4, because B4 needs uninterrupted parking; heightening 4, because A5 consumes all remaining prize time and introduces a concrete bill; specificity 5, because A1's five hours and B4's shift establish the exact constraint; brevity 4, because A5's 'only owe me' follows the arithmetic with understated assurance. Total 92. Five spoken ABABA turns, under 75 words; B can still decline the arrangement. Original firstPass preserved.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The administration is less overtly obstructive than the earlier candidate. Its comic pressure comes from a helpful accommodation revealing how little the prize covers; the hourly price remains unspecified.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S199-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"fb0c1e7f85e092da7e52302f95e72b642e08c7ca776d04f9ffae128863206221\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 invokes their relay signal and A5 their thirty-year tactical understanding, but A3 mainly insists on helping.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3 acknowledges B2's opposition only vaguely; A5 calls B4 a familiar tactic without naming the shared precedent.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 establishes present rivalry; B4 wants an honest race rather than condescending assistance.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5 asserts continued understanding without using it to change a choice or make a more specific interpretation.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies two taps and relay history; B2 makes today's competitive relationship clear.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5's 'each other's tactics' names the theme but leaves the actual recognized tactic unstated.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 68\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Remember our relay signal? Two taps meant you needed me to take over.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"We're racing against each other today. I just want a fair start.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You said that before our county final. You needed a minute to breathe.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"My daughter thinks I'm nervous because she's watching.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"She's missed the ankle bounce. That's your winning leg warming up. I've seen it bring us home.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A uses specific memories of competing together as authority for understanding B's present behavior better than other people do.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Nostalgic loyalty remembers teamwork warmly without claiming that it supplies privileged knowledge of B now.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A3 interprets today's request through the county final; A5 corrects the daughter's reading through a remembered winning movement.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This alternative offers intimate reassurance during a fair rivalry; alternative b claims a private quiz signal overrides outsiders' interpretation of their conduct.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised within the race setting, adding a county-final memory and a daughter's interpretation. Internal second pass: fidelity 5, logic 4, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 5, brevity 4 = 88. B4 introduces a competing reader of B, causing A5 to claim more precise shared knowledge. Five spoken turns, 75 words.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 supplies the ankle bounce verbally without prior mention; actors must make the observation credible through the spoken exchange.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S199-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"258baa86d5df740be0a3aa03967b5d115bfcf4e6c030046ab57ac0b406b19ada\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 invokes an old shared quiz signal; A5 claims outsiders lack the knowledge needed to interpret it.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2's separate teams through enduring mutual recognition; A5 challenges B4's team's interpretation.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 asserts present team boundaries, and B4 introduces obligations to new teammates.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 elevates private understanding above B4's team's right to judge apparent cheating.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes quiz night and an answer signal; B2 places the former teammates on opposing sides.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5's 'know our look well enough' turns B4's moral objection into evidence of outsiders' inferior understanding.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 86\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You're giving me our old quiz-final look.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"We're on different teams. I'm not asking for answers.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I know. Left eyebrow means you have two answers and hate both.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"My captain thinks I've got something in my eye.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"She hasn't sat through your geography rounds. I'll ask for another minute; you found Madagascar in the last one.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A treats remembered quiz partnership as authority to decode B's uncertainty and provide the help B's current captain fails to recognize.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Simple collusion shares answers to gain points without using shared competitive memory as proof of superior personal understanding.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 accepts B2's refusal of answers but diagnoses a specific eyebrow signal; A5 contrasts the captain's eye complaint with a remembered geography recovery and requests thinking time.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a reassures an old teammate about a daughter's interpretation of nerves; this version uses privileged understanding to intervene in the timing of a current opponent's quiz.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised the rejected candidate within opposing quiz teams, adding a specific expression, current captain and remembered last-minute answer. Internal reread under coach-1.0: fidelity 5, because A3 decodes B and A5 grounds superior understanding in their geography rounds; logic 4, because A3 responds to B2's boundary by identifying uncertainty and A5 counters B4's mistaken diagnosis with a request for time, although the current question is unstated; relationship 4, because B2 protects team boundaries while B4 introduces the captain's competing interpretation; heightening 4, because A5 moves from recognizing a look to intervening in the round; specificity 5, because A1's quiz final, A3's eyebrow and A5's Madagascar memory are concrete; brevity 4, because A3's two disliked answers and A5's remembered recovery express the same intimate certainty. Total 88. Five spoken ABABA turns, under 75 words; the host has not granted extra time. Original firstPass preserved.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 assumes additional thinking time will help the present question as it helped before. This is A's confident inference from shared history, and the intervention could still annoy B or the captain.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S200-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"8f5d6a83604162024c8e7f17d92d80036dc5cb97429daed08cf82c69dce80e94\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 explicitly exchanges last year's withdrawal for first song choice; A5 distinguishes gratitude from the advantage still owed.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 reframes B2's unsolicited sacrifice as ease of receipt; A5 treats B4's thanks as payment for only the earlier year.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 contests whether a debt was agreed, and B4 offers gratitude as evidence of having already responded.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 rejects B4's emotional repayment and expressly begins collecting a current opportunity.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 provides a previous audition withdrawal and a concrete present privilege: choosing songs first.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'easy to receive' recasts the objection economically; A5's collection language follows B4's offered repayment.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 90\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I withdrew from last year's audition for you. I get first choice of songs now.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I didn't ask you to withdraw.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Exactly. I made the sacrifice easy to receive.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I thanked you. And I've already rehearsed the only song we both know.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then I'll take that one. You can spend tonight learning something new; I spent last year letting you be heard.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A treats an unsolicited audition sacrifice as a debt that entitles A to take B's prepared song and shift the replacement work onto B.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Guilt-tripping can seek appreciation without collecting a usable advantage at the other person's present expense.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1 demands song priority for the withdrawal; A5 uses B4's preparation to select that exact song and assigns B the cost of learning another.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This alternative converts a missed competitive opportunity into another competitor's preparation burden; alternative b converts past discomfort into both a better room and payment for its upgrade.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised the rejected audition candidate by making B4 disclose preparation that A5 appropriates as repayment. Internal reread under coach-1.0: fidelity 5, because A1 and A5 explicitly exchange the withdrawal for present priority; logic 5, because A3 reframes B2's lack of request and A5 selects B4's known song; relationship 4, because B4 now has rehearsal effort to protect; heightening 4, because A5 exercises priority over a specific prepared choice and imposes replacement work; specificity 4, because A1 establishes auditions 